00:16:54,094 --> 00:16:55,680
How do you guys do code review?

214
00:16:55,840 --> 00:17:03,040
Now and it can be you can do two parts of the question, like before AI, like how do you do code review and then now after AI?

215
00:17:03,360 --> 00:17:05,280
Yes, uh, that's also a very good question.

216
00:17:05,440 --> 00:17:12,240
Before AI, obviously, like I'm super strict on code review, especially when I was at Bloomberg, we were in the security infra team.

217
00:17:12,400 --> 00:17:13,680
That's the most important thing.

218
00:17:13,839 --> 00:17:17,119
Like always look at every single line of code, try to understand everything.

219
00:17:17,280 --> 00:17:19,839
The PR size was also much smaller back then.

220
00:17:19,920 --> 00:17:23,520
Like a few hundred lines of code is already sizable.

221
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:27,396
I cannot imagine anyone giving me like 10,000 lines of code PR.

222
00:17:27,630 --> 00:17:36,400
Whereas here now, sometimes even if you want to divide PRs to be very small, sometimes they can easily be like say thousands of lines of code.

223
00:17:36,560 --> 00:17:40,000
And I now merge maybe I'm not as crazy as some people.

224
00:17:40,080 --> 00:17:43,012
They can merge 100 PR a day.

225
00:17:43,200 --> 00:17:48,548
I usually merge around 10 per day, but that's still way more than I can review.

226
00:17:48,910 --> 00:17:56,720
So I think right now, what I'm thinking more about is actually when we review a PR, what are we reviewing, right?

227
00:17:56,880 --> 00:18:02,960
We are actually just making sure that the code is correct and that there is no bugs, there's no errors.

228
00:18:03,120 --> 00:18:17,600
So I kind of turned code review more into testing reviews because agents on computer use is so good now, and then mostly nowadays I just look at what tests the agent run.

229
00:18:18,030 --> 00:18:33,080
And we also have our own skills to run a very comprehensive test, to test for like user behavior, regression tests, and have separate reviewer, agent reviewers, right, to automatically review for code quality, principles, et cetera.

230
00:18:33,200 --> 00:18:42,720
And to be honest, like what I found right now is also because we usually write all those design principles, coding principles in our repo.

231
00:18:43,120 --> 00:18:49,680
Previously, we still need another agent to enforce okay, the coding agent is actually following those principles.

232
00:18:49,840 --> 00:18:53,440
But now the agents are getting so good we don't even need that.

233
00:18:53,680 --> 00:18:58,724
The coding agent usually just follows all those good principles just in their first try.

234
00:18:58,960 --> 00:19:01,920
So yeah, to maybe recap.

235
00:19:02,702 --> 00:19:18,052
Right now it's mostly through a lot of automated tests, not just unit tests, intuition tests, but agents testing the system as if they're a user using a lot of computer use UI testing uh and also have another agent to review this agent's work.

236
00:19:18,702 --> 00:19:20,132
That's incredible.

237
00:19:20,608 --> 00:19:22,368
Now AI is so powerful.

238
00:19:22,574 --> 00:19:25,408
What are the things that you still don't trust AI to do?

239
00:19:25,488 --> 00:19:37,088
That you still maybe build some rule-based gateway or something like a solution, like pure role-based, or you have yourself as a human to make decision for it.

240
00:19:37,390 --> 00:19:39,728
Yeah, maybe I'll answer this question in two parts.

241
00:19:39,968 --> 00:19:46,048
One part in the development part, and the other part will be right our actual production agent.

242
00:19:46,128 --> 00:19:46,768
What does it do?

243
00:19:47,008 --> 00:19:53,568
I think in terms of development, the number one thing I still don't trust the agent to do is schema design and types design.

244
00:19:53,728 --> 00:20:03,908
I realize that agents are so bad at it, and a lot of times agents like to create some really hacky and bolt-on solutions and just keep building on top of it.

245
00:20:04,208 --> 00:20:13,728
And it never takes a step back to really look at the entire thing as a whole and then see what's the most simple, flexible, robust, elegant solution.

246
00:20:13,888 --> 00:20:20,260
So that's where I still don't trust the agent and I always review all the database schema changes myself.

247
00:20:20,494 --> 00:20:31,008
And to be honest, it also becomes part of my prompt or the skill files I use to always tell the agent, right, to take a step back and think about the entire code base as a whole.

248
00:20:31,408 --> 00:20:39,236
Think about why the solution is simple, flexible, robust, elegant enough that it actually improves the quality of the code base as a whole.

249
00:20:39,488 --> 00:20:41,408
So that's kind of on the development part.

250
00:20:41,728 --> 00:20:48,288
I think on our actual production environment part, there is actually a very simple answer to this.

251
00:20:48,462 --> 00:20:51,088
I don't trust agents to do any mathematics.

252
00:20:51,568 --> 00:21:01,648
So we provide we provide a really good citation system so that the agent can use tools to do all the calculations.

253
00:21:01,808 --> 00:21:14,848
And uh it can and we make sure that say any inputs to any formula are directly cited from the documents or images, etc., that we provide it to our agent as an input.

254
00:21:15,088 --> 00:21:20,208
So the math part, yeah, I definitely still do it using static code.

255
00:21:20,688 --> 00:21:37,568
That's a very interesting point because I think every time there's a new model come out from Antarctic or OpenAI, people are always on Twitter posing about asking a question about some random numbers times another number, and then AI sometimes will give like a random answer, say 47.

256
00:21:37,968 --> 00:21:42,928
And yeah, or sometimes it will give a correct answer and then the user say, oh no, the answer is three.

257
00:21:43,118 --> 00:21:55,408
And then AI is like, oh, you got me, that's three, but it's like 100 times 300, and then AI may say three, which is you know obviously incorrect, but AI, you can kind of tweak the AI to say the wrong answer.

258
00:21:55,488 --> 00:22:00,528
And imagine that happened to your production agent because you guys think the commercial banking.

259
00:22:00,608 --> 00:22:10,928
So if a customer or a user say someone wants to lend 10k, and then your your user say, actually, they want to lend $10, and then everyone said, Oh, you got me $10.

260
00:22:11,408 --> 00:22:13,648
That is so they will be a disaster.

261
00:22:13,888 --> 00:22:14,928
Yeah, definitely, definitely.

262
00:22:15,088 --> 00:22:19,408
Like in any kind of like financial applications, like trust is definitely the most important.

263
00:22:19,568 --> 00:22:34,884
So I think what we build is really kind of like our own domain-specific language for the agent to it's kind of like a tool or infrastructure for the agent to use to make sure that even if we cannot trust the judgment of the agent, we can at least trust those numbers and the calculations.

264
00:22:35,470 --> 00:22:43,204
Since you're a co-founder and the CTO, there's definitely a lot of time that you think you should hire someone to help you.

265
00:22:43,568 --> 00:22:56,848
