00:24:04,592 --> 00:24:10,788
Even though, even if like someone doesn't have a really good judgment, they can still execute and they can provide a lot of value.

281
00:24:11,152 --> 00:24:15,312
For example, like the kind of like junior developers in the past, right?

282
00:24:15,472 --> 00:24:21,152
Senior write sub tickets, think about the design, and then give it to the junior to execute it.

283
00:24:21,518 --> 00:24:25,232
Yeah, and then that part is just now completely automated by AI.

284
00:24:25,472 --> 00:24:34,272
So probably what I'm looking for much more now is also judgment, Whether they have better judgments than AI.

285
00:24:34,574 --> 00:24:36,672
And to be honest, AI has really good judgments.

286
00:24:36,992 --> 00:24:38,192
The bar is very high now.

287
00:24:38,352 --> 00:24:39,748
Yeah, the bar is very high.

288
00:24:40,398 --> 00:24:43,652
And so judgment, I was probably ranking as number one.

289
00:24:43,872 --> 00:24:49,872
And the other say traits that I look for is probably what forms a good judgment.

290
00:24:50,112 --> 00:24:55,232
It could be say experience with AI, right, familiar with the tools, with the models.

291
00:24:55,472 --> 00:25:03,312
So, right, they already understand the beta lesson, or that they have really good in curiosity, super high agency, right?

292
00:25:03,472 --> 00:25:09,152
Just like tries to learn whatever comes out next, always keep themselves up to date.

293
00:25:09,312 --> 00:25:13,552
And yeah, just like generally those trends will be super valuable.

294
00:25:13,712 --> 00:25:24,672
Whereas like someone who's like a grinder but doesn't really like to learn new things and just keeps grinding on the old thing, then that wouldn't have much value in the age of AI, at least to us.

295
00:25:25,102 --> 00:25:27,152
I think the judgment point is interesting.

296
00:25:27,406 --> 00:25:34,272
Is there a way that you have figured out how to tell if someone has a good judgment and then better than AI?

297
00:25:34,672 --> 00:25:34,912
Right.

298
00:25:35,278 --> 00:25:38,948
To be honest, I I don't have a silver bullet there.

299
00:25:39,552 --> 00:25:43,012
what we do when we approach hiring is we just like work trial.

300
00:25:43,232 --> 00:25:59,552
We give them a real task that we're working on right now and have them work on it, have them use their judgment with AI to build something, and then usually after work trial, we will have maybe an hour or two to just go through this design.

301
00:25:59,694 --> 00:26:03,152
And I want to understand, okay, why did you make this decision here?

302
00:26:03,312 --> 00:26:04,832
Why did you make that decision here?

303
00:26:04,992 --> 00:26:05,572
There, right?

304
00:26:06,094 --> 00:26:10,752
A lot of times, to be honest, what I found is they don't even know why those decisions are made.

305
00:26:11,072 --> 00:26:11,820
The AI told them?

306
00:26:11,820 --> 00:26:12,476
Yeah,

307
00:26:12,476 --> 00:26:23,552
They just delegated everything to AI versus I think a really good higher or good candidate would show would be they understand not every single decision, but the most important ones.

308
00:26:23,726 --> 00:26:25,472
They're very conscious about those decisions.

309
00:26:25,632 --> 00:26:27,632
They understand the trade-offs behind it.

310
00:26:27,886 --> 00:26:36,272
And then they can explain to you, okay, because this trade-off, because of that assumption, I assume this is the best, better approach.

311
00:26:36,622 --> 00:26:40,992
Sometimes there's no right or wrong because our assumptions can be different.

312
00:26:41,230 --> 00:26:55,044
But if they can explain a really good reasoning on when they landed on certain design based on the assumptions, trade-offs they thought about, I kind of feel that's the better judgment.

